.TH MACHO\-SYMLOOKUP 1 "October 2, 2026" "MachO-Tools 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
macho\-symlookup \- find which function contains a virtual address
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macho\-symlookup
.I hex-vaddr
.RI [ hex-vaddr ...]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads symbols directly and answers which function contains each given virtual
address, together with the offset into that function and its size.
.PP
This reads the symbol table rather than running
.BR nm (1),
because
.B nm
on a large universal binary takes minutes where this answers in milliseconds.
.PP
Only defined, address\-bearing symbols are considered. Imported symbols carry a
null address, so including them would make any low address resolve to an import
sitting at zero \(em a confident, plausible and wrong answer.
.PP
Every positional argument is an address, so the binary comes from
.B \-b
or from the environment rather than from the last positional.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-arch= name
Prefer one architecture. A preference, not a requirement: an absent architecture
falls back to the richest slice.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-binary " " \fIpath\fR
The binary to read.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON object to standard output and send prose to standard error.
Addresses are rendered as
.B "0x..."
strings rather than numbers.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_BINARY
The binary to read when none is given.
.TP
.B MACHO_APP
An application bundle to read, tried after
.BR MACHO_BINARY .
.TP
.B MACHO_CONFIG
Path to a replacement
.IR config.json .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one address resolved to a function.
.TP
.B 1
The tool ran and nothing resolved. A negative answer, not an error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, including an address that is not hexadecimal with an
.B 0x
prefix.
.TP
.B 3
The file could not be read, or the chosen slice has no symbol table.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
macho\-symlookup 0x100085c30 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go
macho\-symlookup \-\-json 0x100085c30 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go | jq '.data.function'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Note the
.B 0x
prefix. The prefix is required, because a bare hexadecimal number is
ambiguous with a decimal one and a tool that guesses which was meant answers
about the wrong address.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR macho\-sym (1),
.BR macho\-describe (1),
.BR macho\-findcall (1),
.BR nm (1),
.BR otool (1)